WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.860
With certain stitch patterns

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:08.200
like tuck and lace, they require
even weight across your knitting.

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.640
One way to achieve this is
to use a ribber cast on comb.

4
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:17.100
Now, even if you don't have a ribber
attached, the comb can come in handy.

5
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.320
On a single bed, put every other needle

6
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:25.020
into work, leaving the other
needles in out of work position.

7
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.120
Knit across, forming loops.

8
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.020
Carefully push that cast on comb
into the loops that are formed.

9
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.580
Insert the wire in the comb.

10
00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.540
Now, be careful to keep that comb

11
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.220
and the wire in front
of the gate pegs and pull down.

12
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.440
The knitting should look like this.

13
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:56.840
Keeping the comb out of the way
of the carriage, knit across.

14
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:06.520
Then put all the needles
into work and knit a few rows.

15
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.360
Hang some ribber weights as needed.

16
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.020
With this scrap in place,
you can now knit a row of ravel cord

17
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.160
and work a closed cast
on to continue your knitting.

